# Animate

Reveal content when it enters the viewport. The component only observes visibility; every visual transition lives in `animate.css` and works without an animation utility plugin.

## Playground

Build a custom animation, replay it immediately, and copy the generated component usage.

<AnimatePlayground />

## Usage example

<DocsExample title="Animate" source={animateExampleSource}>
	<div class="animate-example">
		<AnimateExample />
	</div>
</DocsExample>

## Variants

Use `fade`, `fade-up`, `fade-down`, `fade-left`, `fade-right`, `zoom`, or `blur`. Numbers passed to `duration` and `delay` are treated as milliseconds; CSS time values such as `0.4s` work too.

```svelte
<AesirAnimate animation="zoom" duration={400} delay={100} easing="out">
	<YourContent />
</AesirAnimate>
```

Set `once={false}` to animate whenever the element re-enters the viewport. `threshold`, `rootMargin`, `distance`, `as`, `class`, and `style` can be adjusted without replacing the component's CSS classes.

The reduced-motion media query disables movement and immediately reveals the content for users who prefer less motion.

<style>
	.animate-example {
		width: 100%;
	}
</style>
